---
import DownloadPicker from "../components/DownloadPicker.svelte";
import { BASE } from "../base";
import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";
import {
  CHROME_STORE_URL,
  DESKTOP_BUILDS,
  EXTENSIONS,
  FIREFOX_ADDON_URL,
  RELEASES,
  REPO_SLUG,
  USERSCRIPT,
  downloadUrl,
} from "../site";

const builds = DESKTOP_BUILDS.map((build) => ({ ...build, url: downloadUrl(build.file) }));
const install = `${BASE}docs/installation/`;
---

<Page
  title="Install"
  description="Add Parousia from the Chrome Web Store or Firefox Add-ons, then download Parousia Desktop for Windows or Linux."
>
  <div class="wrap section">
    <div class="prose">
      <h1>Install Parousia</h1>
      <p>
        Parousia has two parts: the browser extension and Parousia Desktop. Add the extension from
        your browser's store, then download Desktop.
        <a href={install}>Installation</a> covers the first run.
      </p>

      <h2 id="extension">1. Add the extension</h2>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button primary" href={CHROME_STORE_URL}>Chrome Web Store</a>
        <a class="button primary" href={FIREFOX_ADDON_URL}>Firefox Add-ons</a>
      </div>
      <p class="hint">
        Edge and other Chromium browsers install from the Chrome Web Store too. The stores keep the
        extension up to date. No store? <a href="#by-hand">Install by hand</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="desktop">2. Download Parousia Desktop</h2>
      <DownloadPicker client:load builds={builds} />
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>System</th><th>File</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              builds.map((build) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>{build.label}</td>
                  <td><a href={build.url}>{build.file}</a></td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Each is a single file with nothing to install. The Linux builds are static and run on any
        distribution; make the file executable first (<code>chmod +x</code>). There is no macOS
        build yet. Desktop also needs the Discord desktop app running on the same computer.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then open the extension's popup. Firefox installs need one extra step, allowing the
        extension in Desktop, which <a href={`${install}#allow-the-extension-in-desktop`}>Installation</a> shows.
      </p>

      <h2 id="by-hand">Install by hand</h2>
      <p>
        For when a store is not an option. These come from the newest <a href={RELEASES}>release</a>.
      </p>

      <h3>Extension zips</h3>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Browser</th><th>File</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              EXTENSIONS.map((extension) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>{extension.label}</td>
                  <td><a href={downloadUrl(extension.file)}>{extension.file}</a></td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Chromium loads the unzipped folder as an unpacked extension. The Firefox zip is unsigned, so
        Firefox keeps it only until it closes. The steps are in
        <a href={`${install}#install-by-hand`}>Installation</a>.
      </p>

      <h3>Userscript</h3>
      <p>
        For a script manager you already use (Violentmonkey, Tampermonkey, or ScriptCat): <a
          href={downloadUrl(USERSCRIPT.file)}>{USERSCRIPT.file}</a
        >. Desktop accepts a userscript only after you turn on <em>Allow userscripts</em>, because
        that lets any web page reach it.
      </p>

      <h2 id="verify">Check what you downloaded</h2>
      <p>
        Every release has a <code>SHA256SUMS</code> file. Put it next to your downloads and run:
      </p>
      <pre><code>sha256sum --ignore-missing -c SHA256SUMS</code></pre>
      <p>
        On Windows, <code>(Get-FileHash file).Hash</code> in PowerShell gives the value to compare.
        Each file also has a build provenance attestation, which shows the workflow run that built
        it:
      </p>
      <pre><code>{`gh attestation verify parousia-desktop-linux-x86_64 --repo ${REPO_SLUG}`}</code></pre>
    </div>
  </div>
</Page>
